        "problem": "Normalize the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$",
        "worked_solution": "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{\u2016x\u2016}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $\u2016x\u2016$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016x\u2016 & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\u2016x\u2016} & =\\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-2}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
